# level8 → level9

The command loop allocates only four bytes for `auth`, but `login` reads
four bytes at `auth + 0x20`. `service` stores a copy of its argument in a
separate heap allocation. On the VM, `auth` starts at `0x0804a008` and
`service` at `0x0804a018`, so `auth+0x20` points 16 bytes into service's
controllable string.

From the level8 account on the RainFall VM, enter each line interactively:

```text
./level8
auth a
service AAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAA
login
id
cat /home/user/level9/.pass
```

The 32 `A`s make the integer checked by `login` nonzero, so it executes
`/bin/sh` with level9 effective UID. The program's printed heap pointers
show the allocation spacing. If piping input, send the shell commands
after `login` has executed; `fgets` may otherwise buffer those lines before
the child shell starts. `disassemble main` shows the `malloc(4)`,
`strdup`, and read at offset `0x20`.
